<.guide_page
  signed_in?={@signed_in?}
  title={@guide_meta.title}
  dek={@guide_meta.dek}
  date={@guide_meta.date}
  read_time={@guide_meta.read_time}
>
  <p>
    Imagine Grafana sends you an alert about lock waits on your billing database.
    You ask an AI agent to find out what’s blocking the updates. Order 42 is already
    paid, but the update that would mark it as sent is stuck. One query shows which
    PostgreSQL session holds the lock. A second shows why it still holds it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A PostgreSQL query was enough to start here. On the next alert, the agent might
    need to check Redis or look up a Sentry event. You want those tools available
    when they’re needed. But some MCP clients put every tool’s name, description,
    and arguments in the prompt before the agent makes its first call. That uses
    tokens on every investigation, and a long list can make it harder to pick the
    right tool.
  </p>
  <p>
    Emisar gives the agent 14 MCP tools to start with. One of them, <code>find_actions</code>, lets it search for actions it can run on the hosts it
    can access. We’ll compare the cost of loading every action up front with the
    cost of looking them up as needed, then follow the agent through the order 42
    investigation to see where the search helps and where it can miss.
  </p>

  <h2 id="token-count">What goes into the first prompt?</h2>
  <p>
    We took the catalog as it stood at the time of writing and treated each action
    as a separate MCP tool. We counted the tokens in each tool’s name, description, and full argument
    schema. Then we used the same tokenizer on Emisar’s actual <code>tools/list</code> response.
  </p>
  <figure class="my-8">
    <figcaption class="mb-4 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">
      Tokens in tool definitions before the first call
    </figcaption>
    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm leading-5 text-zinc-300">
      <thead class="border-b border-zinc-700">
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="pb-3 pr-3 font-medium">Available actions</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-2 pb-3 text-right font-medium">One tool per action</th>
          <th scope="col" class="pb-3 pl-2 text-right font-medium text-brand-300">emisar</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-800 tabular-nums">
        <tr :for={
          {scope, actions, eager} <- [
            {"PostgreSQL", "49 actions", "7,158"},
            {"Production stack", "346 actions across 12 packs", "64,742"},
            {"Full catalog", "1,918 actions across 108 packs", "391,423"}
          ]
        }>
          <th scope="row" class="py-3.5 pr-3 font-normal">
            <span class="block text-zinc-100">{scope}</span>
            <span class="text-xs text-zinc-400">{actions}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="px-2 text-right">{eager}</td>
          <td class="pl-2 text-right font-semibold text-brand-300">10,572</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </figure>
  <p>
    If PostgreSQL is all you need, loading its 49 tools directly saves 3,414 tokens up
    front. In Emisar’s list, the two tools for creating and editing runbooks account
    for 4,582 tokens, even if all you need is one database read.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the 12-pack example, we included the tools you might need on the host
    itself (Linux Core, Docker, Systemd Deep, Debugging, and Network TLS), plus
    Kubernetes, Nginx, PostgreSQL, Redis, Grafana, Prometheus, and Sentry. Loading
    all 346 actions up front takes 64,742 tokens. Emisar’s tool list takes 10,572. For the full catalog, that’s <strong>97.3% fewer tokens in the initial tool definitions</strong>.
    If your client loads tools on demand, check which definitions it sends in the first
    request. <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/advanced-tool-use">Anthropic’s
    five-server example</a> shows how much the count can change.
  </p>

  <h2 id="selection">Why fewer tools can help</h2>
  <p>
    Tool descriptions take up space in the prompt. A long list can also make it harder
    for the model to pick the right tool. Anthropic’s
    <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/tool-search-tool">tool search docs</a>
    say Claude has more trouble choosing once it has more than 30 to 50 tools.
    One study found a difference even with just five tools.
  </p>
  <figure class="my-8">
    <figcaption class="mb-4 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">
      Task success with fewer tools in the prompt
    </figcaption>
    <table class="w-full text-left text-sm leading-5 text-zinc-300">
      <thead class="border-b border-zinc-700">
        <tr>
          <th scope="col" class="pb-3 pr-3 font-medium">Measurement</th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-2 pb-3 text-right font-medium">More tools</th>
          <th scope="col" class="pb-3 pl-2 text-right font-medium">Fewer tools</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-800 tabular-nums">
        <tr :for={
          {source, href, detail, more, fewer} <- [
            {"Anthropic MCP evaluations, Claude Opus 4",
             "https://www.anthropic.com/research/advanced-tool-use",
             "All tools loaded vs. tool search", "49%", "74%"},
            {"Anthropic MCP evaluations, Claude Opus 4.5",
             "https://www.anthropic.com/research/advanced-tool-use",
             "All tools loaded vs. tool search", "79.5%", "88.1%"},
            {"MCPVerse task success, Claude Sonnet 4", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.16260",
             "All 65 servers and 550+ tools vs. the servers relevant to each question", "44.2%",
             "62.4%"},
            {"Shortlist study, Claude Sonnet 4.6", "https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.24660",
             "A fixed list of 5 tools vs. an adaptive list averaging about 2", "87.1%", "93.1%"}
          ]
        }>
          <th scope="row" class="py-3.5 pr-3 font-normal">
            <a href={href} class="block text-zinc-100">{source}</a>
            <span class="text-xs text-zinc-400">{detail}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="px-2 text-right">{more}</td>
          <td class="pl-2 text-right font-semibold text-zinc-100">{fewer}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </figure>
  <p>
    In MCPVerse, loading every server took about 147,000 tokens just for tool
    descriptions. In the five-tool study, the shorter list helped most on harder
    questions where the right tool appeared farther down the list. On those questions,
    the success rate rose from 60.9% to 76.8%.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/tool-space-interference-in-the-mcp-era-designing-for-agent-compatibility-at-scale/">Microsoft Research</a>
    found that tools can work well on their own and still be easy to mix up when
    they have similar names or do similar jobs. In our catalog,
    <code>postgres.lock_blocking_chains</code>
    and <code>mysql.lock_waits</code>
    both
    look for locks, but in different databases. With <code>find_actions</code>, the
    model searches the catalog and sees only the matches. The next example shows what
    happens when the search misses the action it needs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="discovery">Example of a frontier model finding the lock</h2>
  <p>
    To find what’s holding up order 42, we asked a frontier model to look for a
    PostgreSQL lock query. It searched a local workspace with all 108 packs across
    15 test runners using <code>find_actions</code>. When the model passed “which
    postgres session is holding up an update” as the search query, the right action
    didn’t make the top three. With “blocked queries waiting on locks” as the search
    query, <code>find_actions</code>
    returned <code>postgres.lock_blocking_chains</code>
    first. The results also included a
    MySQL action. If you already know the database, set <code>pack_id</code>
    to <code>postgres</code>
    to search just that pack.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model looked up the PostgreSQL action with <code>get_action</code>. The
    action needed no query arguments, and <code>pg-primary</code>
    could run it.
    On PostgreSQL 17.12, using a monitoring role, the query found both sessions.
    <code>postgres.activity_detail</code>
    showed what the session holding the lock
    was doing.
  </p>
  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Order 42 · selected fields">Waiting   198   UPDATE orders SET state = 'sent' WHERE id = 42;
Holding   186   UPDATE orders SET state = 'paid' WHERE id = 42;
State     186   idle in transaction</.docs_code>
  <p>
    Session 186 had finished its update and left the transaction open. The lock
    would stay until the transaction ended. We rolled it back from session 186.
    The waiting update finished, order 42 was marked sent, and the lock query
    came back empty. You can <.link href={~p"/packs/postgres#postgres-ai-example"}>try this in three psql sessions</.link>.
  </p>
  <figure class="my-8 border-y border-zinc-800 py-6" aria-labelledby="lookup-cost">
    <figcaption id="lookup-cost" class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
      Tool tokens before the first database read
    </figcaption>
    <p>
      Loading all 346 actions in the 12-pack stack as separate tools would take
      <strong>64,742 tokens</strong>
      up front.
      For order 42, the fixed tool list, one search result, and the details for the
      lock query came to <strong>12,145 tokens</strong>.
    </p>
    <div
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="mt-5 grid h-3 grid-cols-[10572fr_1242fr_331fr] gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-sm"
    >
      <span class="bg-zinc-500"></span>
      <span class="bg-brand-500"></span>
      <span class="bg-brand-200"></span>
    </div>
    <dl class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-3 gap-3 text-sm">
      <div>
        <dt class="text-zinc-400">Fixed tool list</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-zinc-100">10,572</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="text-zinc-400">Search result</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-brand-400">1,242</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="text-zinc-400">Action detail</dt>
        <dd class="mt-1 font-mono text-brand-200">331</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </figure>
  <p>
    The 12,145-token total counts the full search result and action details once each. If your
    client sends both the structured result and a text copy, count both. Tool-call
    inputs, conversation history, and the database output are outside this total.
  </p>
  <details class="group my-6 border-y border-zinc-800">
    <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer list-none items-center gap-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-zinc-200 hover:text-white [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
      <span
        aria-hidden="true"
        class="ml-0.5 size-1.5 shrink-0 -rotate-45 border-r border-b border-current text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-45"
      ></span>
      Run the search from the CLI
    </summary>
    <.docs_code
      phx-no-format
      label="Find the lock query"
      copy_text={"emisar-mcp find_actions '{\n  \"query\": \"blocked queries waiting on locks\",\n  \"limit\": 3\n}' --json > matches.json\njq -r '.candidates[].action_id' matches.json"}
    >emisar-mcp find_actions '&#123;
  "query": "blocked queries waiting on locks",
  "limit": 3
}' --json &gt; matches.json
jq -r '.candidates[].action_id' matches.json

postgres.lock_blocking_chains
gcp.sql_query_insights
mysql.lock_waits</.docs_code>
    <p>
      The search result includes a <code>next</code> call with the action ID and
      exact pack hash. Pass those arguments to <code>get_action</code> to see what
      the action needs and which hosts can run it.
    </p>
    <.docs_code
      phx-no-format
      label="Check the arguments and host"
      copy_text={"jq '.candidates[] | select(.action_id == \"postgres.lock_blocking_chains\")\n    | .next.arguments' matches.json |\n  emisar-mcp get_action - --json > action.json\njq '{risk: .action.risk, args: .action.args_schema.properties,\n    targets: [.compatible_runners[].name]}' action.json"}
    >jq '.candidates[] | select(.action_id == "postgres.lock_blocking_chains")
    | .next.arguments' matches.json |
  emisar-mcp get_action - --json &gt; action.json
jq '&#123;risk: .action.risk, args: .action.args_schema.properties,
    targets: [.compatible_runners[].name]}' action.json

&#123;
  "risk": "medium",
  "args": &#123;},
  "targets": ["pg-primary"]
}</.docs_code>
  </details>

  <details id="measure" class="group my-6 border-y border-zinc-800">
    <summary class="flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer list-none items-center gap-2.5 text-sm font-medium text-zinc-200 hover:text-white [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
      <span
        aria-hidden="true"
        class="ml-0.5 size-1.5 shrink-0 -rotate-45 border-r border-b border-current text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-45"
      ></span>
      Measure your own MCP setup
    </summary>
    <p>
      Collect the <code>tools</code>
      arrays from every page of each server’s <code>tools/list</code>
      response and combine them into one JSON array. For Emisar,
      sign in and run <code>emisar-mcp list_tools --json</code>
      to get the array. The
      script below counts tokens with <code>o200k_base</code>. If your client rewrites
      descriptions or loads tools on demand, also count the tool definitions the client
      actually sends in its first request to the model.
    </p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="count_tools.py" copy_id="count-tools-python">import json
import sys
import tiktoken

with open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8") as source:
    tools = json.load(source)
text = json.dumps(tools, ensure_ascii=False,
                  sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":"))
tokens = tiktoken.get_encoding("o200k_base").encode_ordinary(text)
print(f"&#123;len(tools):,} tools, &#123;len(tokens):,} tokens")</.docs_code>
    <h3>Reproduce the catalog comparison</h3>
    <p>
      We used the
      <a href="https://github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/tree/21ae3e38a6a58b32844b42d0388b218ba471eb5e">November 24 source snapshot</a>
      and Python’s <code>tiktoken</code>
      0.14.0. In a configured checkout of that revision,
      save this file in <code>portal/</code>
      and run <code>mix run --no-start snapshot.exs</code>
      there. It writes one JSON array per row and the fixed MCP list. Pass each file to the
      counter above. Running <code>python count_tools.py stack.json</code>
      prints <code>346 tools, 64,742 tokens</code>.
    </p>
    <.docs_code phx-no-format label="snapshot.exs" copy_id="snapshot-elixir">alias EmisarWeb.MCP.&#123;SchemaRegistry, ToolSchema}

packs = Jason.decode!(File.read!("apps/emisar/priv/packs/catalog.json"))["packs"]
for &#123;file, ids} &lt;- [&#123;"postgres", ["postgres"]},
                    &#123;"stack", ~w(postgres kubernetes redis sentry linux-core docker systemd-deep
                                  debugging network-tls nginx grafana prometheus)},
                    &#123;"catalog", :all}] do
  tools = for pack &lt;- packs, ids == :all or pack["id"] in ids,
              action &lt;- pack["actions"] do
    schema = ToolSchema.action_args_schema(%&#123;args_schema: %&#123;"args" =&gt; action["args"] || []}})
    %&#123;name: action["id"], description: action["description"], inputSchema: schema}
  end
  File.write!(file &lt;&gt; ".json", Jason.encode!(tools))
end
File.write!("emisar.json", Jason.encode!(SchemaRegistry.tools()))</.docs_code>
    <p>
      Pick a question you ask regularly and try each setup in a fresh session with the
      same model. Compare the first requests, then add up the input tokens across all
      requests through the answer. That total includes what it cost to search again or
      send a whole result table back to the model.
    </p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="takeaway">When to load tools on demand</h2>
  <p>
    If the agent only needs PostgreSQL, loading its tools directly costs fewer
    tokens up front. With a larger catalog, a short starting list leaves more
    room in the prompt and gives the model fewer tools to choose from.
  </p>
</.guide_page>
